When you click a region defined by an `<area>` (over the top of an `<img>`), Firefox and Safari consider the `img` element to be `:active`, whereas Chrome considers the `<area>` to be `:active`.

Some testcases are attached in https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1980243 , particularly this one:
https://bug1980243.bmoattachments.org/attachment.cgi?id=9507782

The testcase has this style-rule to reveal when you're hovering the `<area>` with ID `head`, which defines a region around the dog's head:
```css
#head:hover {
  cursor: grab;
}
```
This^ successfully changes the cursor when you hover the dog's head in Firefox and Chrome, but not Safari.

The testcase has these style-rules to reveal which element is considered `:active`
```css
#head:active {
cursor: grabbing;
}
img:active { outline: 25px solid red }
```

When you click the dog's head:
* in Chrome, the cursor changes to a "grabbing" clenched hand (showing that the `<area>` (`#head`) is `:active`.
* in Firefox (and Safari), the `<img>` gains a red outline (showing that the `<img>` is `:active`)

Notably, Chrome *does not* add any outline to the `<img>` when you click the dog's head; so Chrome is adding `:active` to *just* the `<area>`, not to the `<img>`.  But it does add a red outline if you click elsewhere on the image.

I don't have strong opinions about what should happen here at this point, but I'll note that `:active` is mostly useful for showing **visual feedback** about what was clicked, and `<area>` is not a great element for doing that, since it's essentially a non-rendering element (it doesn't render its borders or backgrounds or anything else visual; maybe (?) only `cursor` has a visible effect).  So that makes me weakly favor the Firefox behavior.
